_SAFE = f"""<h2>Fire safety on any collapsible grill</h2>{ul(["<b>Outdoors only.</b> Burning charcoal makes carbon monoxide, which you can't see or smell. Never use it in a garage, tent, camper, vehicle, boat cabin or under a roof or awning.","<b>Non-combustible, level ground.</b> Soil, gravel, sand, concrete, pavers or a fire-safe pad.","<b>Clearance.</b> Away from walls, vehicles, tents, overhanging branches and dry grass. Kids and pets kept back.","<b>Seated tabs.</b> Check every tab is fully in its slot before you add fuel.","<b>Water and gloves at hand.</b> Hot steel looks exactly like cold steel.","<b>Leave it alone until cold.</b> Don't move or take apart a lit or warm grill."])}"""
_SIZE = f"""<h2>The two Slot Grills at a glance</h2>{table(["","Compact","Full Size"],[["Steel","12-gauge carbon steel","10-gauge carbon steel"],["Cooking area","16.25&quot; × 9&quot;","20.5&quot; × 11.25&quot;"],["Weight, grill only","About 14 lb","About 27.6 lb"],["Packs to","About 1&quot;","About 1.25&quot;"],["Air gap","2⅞&quot;","3⅝&quot;"]])}<p>Figures from SlotGrill.com. For the full breakdown, see {L("compact-vs-full-size-slot-grill","Compact vs Full Size")}.</p>"""
_CARE = f"""<h2>The one-minute care habit</h2><p>Most carbon steel problems come from skipping the last minute of a cook. After the grill cools, scrape the grates, empty the ash, and wipe a whisper-thin coat of oil or <a href="{BALM}">Seasoning Balm</a> on the cooking surfaces. Store it dry. That habit is the difference between a grill that darkens into a slick patina and one that shows orange freckles every spring. If freckles do show up, it's not a disaster: see {L("surface-rust-on-carbon-steel","surface rust")}.</p>"""
_MISTAKE = f"""<h2>Mistakes new flat-pack owners make</h2>{ol(["<b>Too much charcoal.</b> A small firebox gets very hot. Start with half a chimney and add.","<b>No cool zone.</b> Without an empty end, a flare-up has nowhere to go.","<b>Cooking on black coals.</b> Wait for gray ash.","<b>Old ash left in.</b> It blocks airflow; empty it before every cook.","<b>Heavy oil when seasoning.</b> Thin coats cure; thick coats turn gummy.","<b>Packing it warm.</b> Wait until steel and ash are cold, every time."])}"""
_COOK = f"""<h2>Cooking notes for a collapsible grill</h2><p>A collapsible grill puts food close to the coals, so things brown faster than on a tall backyard cooker. Pat food dry, oil the food rather than the grate when you can, and leave it alone until it releases. Keep a clean platter for cooked food, separate from the one that carried raw meat. And cook to temperature, not time: wind, charcoal type and how full the grill is all change the clock.</p>"""
